  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 210 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nMonday through Saturday:\n\nHigh pressure gradually sinks builds across the Lower \nMississippi Valley on Monday, turning winds southwesterly and \nbringing a return to highs around 60 degrees. A strong 500 mb \nshortwave moving through central Ontario will allow for winds to\nremain gusty into the evening hours before diminishing. A weak \ncold front will follow, bringing northwest winds on Tuesday. \nStill anticipating temperatures around 60 degrees Tuesday \nafternoon before reinforcing cold high pressure builds in \nTuesday evening.\n\nWednesday, another shortwave propagating through the mean flow \naloft looks to move southeastward through the Canadian \nPrairies/northern Plains and bring a few chances for rain \nshowers (15-20%) as well as highs in the low 50s with plenty of \ncloud cover. With this less intense shortwave, model uncertainty\nwith placement remains. Euro is the outlier at this time, \nshowing a phasing, deepening system across Illinois as the \nshortwave swings farther south and interacts with warm, moist \nair from the Gulf, while GFS/Canadian indicate a weaker system \nblending into the larger semi-stationary low over the Canadian \nMaritimes. Kept NBM PoPs for this run due to this uncertainty, \nbut confidence in some kind of scattered showers is increasing.\n\nMoving into Thursday, models have converged on a period of \nnorth to northeast flow, bringing large fetch and lake effect \nshowers from Lake Michigan throughout the day through the \novernight hours and perhaps as late as Friday night before winds\nshift north to northwest and bring an end to precipitation. \nWith Lake Michigan water temperatures still in the 50s and \ninland temperatures falling into the mid to low 30s inland, a \nmix of rain and snow is possible. At this time, not anticipating\na transition to pure snow, but colder trends will be monitored.\nDuration of lake effect precipitation and cold air potential \nwill be dependent on the depth and placement of a developing \nNor'easter along the Atlantic coastal states, with a stronger, \nfarther north solution being the best for longer duration fetch \nthrough Friday night. Outside of lake effect regions, expect dry\nand cloudy conditions Thursday and Friday with highs in the \nmid-40s and lows around 30 degrees.\n\nHigh pressure returns for Saturday, with northwest winds and \nplenty of sunshine. Expect highs around 50 degrees.\n\nMH",
